From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: uart1 on PG6/PG7 as the distinguishing pinctrl proof

`arm64-dts-allwinner-add-A733-minimal-boot-dt`'s uart0 pin node cannot be proven by a boot. U-Boot muxes PB9/PB10
before the kernel starts, so the console riding uart0 cannot tell our
pinctrl's write from the inherited state. This
patch adds a pin group that U-Boot never touches, as the cheaper
variant of the proof: uart1 on PG6/PG7, consumed by a
dw-apb-uart node exactly like uart0 is.

Values, each from the A733 documentation:
- UART1 at 0x02501000-0x02501FFF (A733 user manual). The node maps 0x400, as uart0's node in `arm64-dts-allwinner-add-A733-minimal-boot-dt` does: the
  UART register list (UM 18.10.8) ends at UART_FCC, 0x00F0.
- IRQ: INTID 35 = GIC_SPI 3.
- PG6 selector 2 = UART1-TX, PG7 selector 2 =
  UART1-RX (A733 user manual).
- CLK_UART1 and RST_BUS_UART1 come from `clk-sunxi-ng-add-A733-sun60iw2-minimal-ccu`: UART1_BGR_REG at 0x0E04,
  gate bit 0, reset bit 16 (A733 user manual).
- PG6 (ball T1) and PG7 (ball V2) (A733 datasheet V0.93, ball map) carry
  no-connect marks in the Cubie A7S schematic V1.10, page 7. Nothing on
  the board sees TX; RX has nothing attached. The group sets no bias, as
  uart0's does, so PG7's input floats on an open pad: nothing reads it
  while ttyS1 is closed, and the proof stays the mux write alone.

A523 value rejected: the A523 puts uart1 at 0x02500400
(sun55i-a523.dtsi), and so does U-Boot's own sun60i-a733.dtsi in the
pinned u-boot-dlan17 tree, where the node stays disabled. On the A733
0x02500400 lies inside UART0's 4 KiB window; the UM places UART1 at
0x02501000.

How Linux reads it (6.18.36):
- Every property is read by somebody: reg-shift and reg-io-width by
  __uart_read_properties() (serial_port.c); clocks by dw8250_probe()
  through devm_clk_get_optional_enabled(dev, NULL); resets by
  devm_reset_control_array_get_optional_exclusive(); pinctrl-0 by
  pinctrl_bind_pins() (drivers/base/pinctrl.c, called from really_probe()
  before the driver's probe); pins, function and allwinner,pinmux by
  pinctrl-sunxi-dt.c.
- The serial1 alias is not decoration. dw8250_probe() zeroes its port
  and only an alias sets port->line, so without it uart1's line is 0,
  and serial8250_find_match_or_unused() tries that slot first. Should
  uart1 ever probe before uart0, it would become ttyS0, and
  console=ttyS0 would land on no-connect pins. With the alias the line is
  1 by construction.
- VCC-PG has no traced rail yet, so no vcc-pg-supply is wired. Like PB, PH, PL and PM, the first request on PG logs "supply vcc-pg not found,
  using dummy regulator" and "Couldn't set bank PG I/O bias: -22". The
  dummy regulator returns before the bias register is written, and the
  request only warns (`pinctrl-sunxi-a733-2-bit-pow-mod-sel-io-bias`), so the PG bias stays as boot left it and the
  mux is still written.

Why this is a proof with a falsifier. PG_CFG0 (0x02000380) resets to
0xFFFFFFFF; PG6 is bits
[27:24], PG7 bits [31:28].
  1. At the U-Boot prompt, before booting: `md.l 0x02000380 1` must read
     0xff in bits [31:24]. Anything else means the boot chain touched PG6
     or PG7: record it, and the run proves nothing.
  2. After boot: `devmem 0x02000380` must read 0x22 in bits [31:24]. Only
     our pinctrl can have written that.
  Bits [23:0] (PG0-PG5) are not part of the proof in either step.
  Also read UART1_BGR (0x02002E04): its reset bit 16 is
  deasserted only by dw8250_probe(), which runs after the pin state was
  applied, so PG6/PG7 still io-disabled with that bit set is a FAIL: the
  kernel believes it muxed the pins and the register says otherwise.

What it proves: the consumer path uart0 relies on -- pinctrl-0 on a
dw-apb-uart node, through pinctrl-sunxi-dt.c -- writes the A733 mux
register at the right bank and field. For `arm64-dts-allwinner-add-A733-minimal-boot-dt`'s own PB9/PB10 node that
is transitive (same path, another bank); a direct test of that node is still separate.

Not hardware-proven: the two readbacks above are still owed.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ / {
 	aliases {
 		ethernet0 = &gmac0;
 		serial0 = &uart0;
+		serial1 = &uart1;
 	};
 
 	chosen {
@@ -761,6 +762,12 @@ uart0_pb_pins: uart0-pb-pins {
 				function = "uart0";
 			};
 
+			uart1_pg_pins: uart1-pg-pins {
+				pins = "PG6", "PG7";
+				allwinner,pinmux = <2>;
+				function = "uart1";
+			};
+
 			/*
 			 * SD splits CLK from CMD+data because the vendor drives
 			 * them differently, and the difference is measurable on
@@ -1119,6 +1126,27 @@ uart0: serial@2500000 {
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * Not a console and no user: this node is the pinctrl proof that
+		 * uart0 cannot give. U-Boot muxes PB9/PB10 before we boot, so a
+		 * boot never tells our write from U-Boot's. PG6/PG7 are no-connect
+		 * pins U-Boot leaves at their reset value, so the UART1 selector
+		 * (mux 2) on them after boot was written by our pinctrl. Procedure:
+		 * the header of patch `arm64-dts-a733-uart1-on-pg6-pg7-pinctrl-proof`.
+		 */
+		uart1: serial@2501000 {
+			compatible = "snps,dw-apb-uart";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02501000 0x0 0x400>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 3 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			reg-shift = <2>;
+			reg-io-width = <4>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_UART1>;
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_UART1>;
+			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			pinctrl-0 = <&uart1_pg_pins>;
+			status = "okay";
+		};
+
 		mmc0: mmc@4020000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-mmc",
 				     "allwinner,sun20i-d1-mmc";
